<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Home Church, privacy manifest.

  Required since spring 2024 for apps and for third party SDKs. Xcode reads
  this, plus the manifest inside every embedded framework, and produces the
  privacy report you attach to a submission.

  WHERE THIS GOES. Once `npx cap add ios` has generated ios/, copy this to
  ios/App/App/PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy and make sure it is a member of the App
  target in Xcode:

      cp ios-config/PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy ios/App/App/

  It lives in ios-config/ rather than in ios/ because ios/ is generated and
  gitignored, and a hand written file inside a regenerable directory is a file
  that gets destroyed the first time somebody runs `npx cap add ios` again.

  VERIFY IT IN XCODE BEFORE SUBMITTING. Apple's own required reason API page
  would not load when this was written, so the reason code below comes from
  Apple Developer Forums rather than first hand from the documentation. Xcode
  reports the codes it actually expects, and an ITMS-91053 warning on upload
  will name anything missing. Do not take this file on faith.

  WHAT THIS APP DOES NOT DO, which is most of it. No analytics, no crash
  reporting, no advertising, no attribution, no third party SDK other than
  Capacitor itself. That is why NSPrivacyTracking is false and the tracking
  domain list is empty, and it is worth keeping that way: every one of those
  additions would need its own signed manifest and its own line on the
  App Store privacy label.

  KEEP THIS FILE AND SUBMISSION_KIT.md SECTION 5 SAYING THE SAME THING. Xcode
  builds the privacy report from this manifest, and the App Store privacy
  label is typed by hand into App Store Connect. They are two copies of one
  answer, and a reviewer comparing them is comparing a report Apple generated
  against a label we claimed. `npm run preflight` fails when the two lists
  disagree, because this file went stale once already: it was written for an
  app with no accounts and still said so long after sign in went live and
  began syncing a home address to our server.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>

  <!-- Nothing here links user or device data across other companies' apps or
       websites. No ATT prompt should ever appear in this app. If one does,
       something was added that should not have been. -->
  <key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
  <false/>

  <key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
  <array/>

  <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
  <array>

    <!-- The APNs device token, so the church can say a new guide is posted.
         Not linked, and that word is doing real work now that accounts exist:
         `device_tokens` is keyed on the token itself and carries no account
         column, so a token cannot be walked back to a person. Migration 0010
         is what holds it there, and 0012 added only per-topic switches beside
         it. If a future migration puts a user id on that table, this entry
         becomes Linked and so does the App Store label. -->
    <dict>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
      <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeDeviceID</string>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
      <false/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
      <false/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
      <array>
        <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
      </array>
    </dict>

    <!-- Everything from here down to Other User Content has two sources, and
         only the first one existed when this file was written.

         SIGNING IN, which is the substantial one. Your information syncs to
         `public.profiles` through FIELD_MAP in js/auth.js the moment somebody
         signs in: first and last name, email, birthday, gender, campus,
         marital status, and a street address. Filling any of it in is
         optional and the app works without, but the fields are there and the
         sync is real, so all of it is declared.

         THE CONNECT FORMS, which is the older and smaller one. Those open in
         the system browser and post to Church Center, Group Vitals, Flodesk,
         or a Google form, so the app never handles the values itself. Apple
         offers an optional disclosure exemption for data a person volunteers
         in a customer service context and these plausibly qualify, but
         over-declaring costs nothing and under-declaring is a metadata
         rejection.

         `photo_url` is in FIELD_MAP and is deliberately NOT declared as
         Photos or Videos. Nothing in the app writes it and no screen shows
         it: it is a column with no path to it. Build one and this manifest
         gains an entry. -->
    <dict>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
      <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeName</string>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
      <true/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
      <false/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
      <array>
        <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
      </array>
    </dict>

    <!-- The street, unit, city, state, and zip under Your information. Five
         columns on `profiles`, optional, and the reason the attorney review
         in LAUNCH_TODO.md stopped being theoretical: a church app collects
         home addresses from a congregation that includes minors. -->
    <dict>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
      <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePhysicalAddress</string>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
      <true/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
      <false/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
      <array>
        <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
      </array>
    </dict>

    <!-- Birthday, gender, campus, and marital status. Apple's Sensitive Info
         category is a fixed list (race, sexual orientation, pregnancy,
         disability, religious or philosophical belief, union membership,
         political opinion, genetic, biometric) and none of these four are on
         it, so Other Data is where they belong. Religious belief is not
         collected from anybody: this is a church's app, which says something
         about the publisher and nothing about a field in it. -->
    <dict>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
      <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeOtherDataTypes</string>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
      <true/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
      <false/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
      <array>
        <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
      </array>
    </dict>

    <!-- The Supabase auth uuid. It is the primary key of the profile row and
         the value every row level security policy compares against, so it is
         unambiguously collected and unambiguously linked. -->
    <dict>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
      <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeUserID</string>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
      <true/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
      <false/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
      <array>
        <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
      </array>
    </dict>

    <!-- What sign in is built on, so it is the one field here that is not
         optional. -->
    <dict>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
      <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeEmailAddress</string>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
      <true/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
      <false/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
      <array>
        <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
      </array>
    </dict>

    <!-- Declared because classify() in js/auth.js still accepts a phone
         number and would create a phone account on it. No SMS provider is
         connected, so that path fails today. The code path exists, so the
         declaration does. -->
    <dict>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
      <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePhoneNumber</string>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
      <true/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
      <false/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
      <array>
        <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
      </array>
    </dict>

    <!-- This one grew, and the part that grew is the substantial one.

         WHAT PEOPLE WRITE IN A GROUP ROOM. Answers to discussion questions
         and prayer requests are stored on our server under the author's first
         name and shown to the other people in that room. It is the only place
         in the app where something a person typed is shown to another person,
         which is also why the age rating answers User Generated Content with
         Yes. Deleted with the account, and swept ninety days after the room
         was opened.

         A JOURNAL ENTRY, once signed in. Private to its author and enforced
         by row level security rather than by a screen, but it is on our
         server, so it is collected.

         THE CONNECT FORMS, the original reason this entry existed.

         Guide notes, question checkmarks, the leader's roster, attendance
         marks, and locally saved prayer requests are NOT part of this answer.
         They never leave the device, and Apple does not ask you to declare
         what does not leave. -->
    <dict>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
      <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeOtherUserContent</string>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
      <true/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
      <false/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
      <array>
        <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
      </array>
    </dict>

  </array>

  <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
  <array>

    <!-- Capacitor's iOS layer reads and writes UserDefaults for its own
         configuration. Our JavaScript uses localStorage rather than
         UserDefaults, but ITMS-91053 keys off what is in the binary, not what
         language it was written in, so this is declared deliberately rather
         than left to be discovered on upload.

         CA92.1: access to information stored in user defaults that is
         accessible only to the app itself. It does not permit reading what
         other apps or the system wrote, and we do not. -->
    <dict>
      <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
      <string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
      <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
      <array>
        <string>CA92.1</string>
      </array>
    </dict>

    <!-- @capacitor/filesystem writes two kinds of temporary file into the
         app's own cache directory: an .ics when somebody adds an event to
         their calendar, and an .html when a leader saves a guide. Both are
         handed straight to the share sheet and neither is kept.

         C617.1: access to file timestamps and other metadata for files inside
         the app container, the app group container, or the app's CloudKit
         container. It does not cover reading timestamps of files elsewhere on
         the device, and we do not.

         Declared defensively. Capacitor's plugins ship their own manifests
         and this may already be covered by the framework's, in which case
         this entry is harmless duplication. Xcode's privacy report will say
         which, and that check is on the launch list. -->
    <dict>
      <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
      <string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp</string>
      <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
      <array>
        <string>C617.1</string>
      </array>
    </dict>

  </array>

</dict>
</plist>
